y in Touraine, his life spanned the tumultuous mid-14th century (died 1367-03-23), an era defined by Anglo-French conflict and the devastating impact of the Black Death. His strategic acumen and martial prowess were instrumental in defending French interests against English incursions, establishing him as a key figure in the Capetian and early Valois military aristocracy.

Boucicaut's military service began early, participating in campaigns that aimed to consolidate French royal authority and repel English advances. He distinguished himself in various skirmishes and sieges, including the defense of the French frontier in Picardy and Normandy. His rise through the ranks culminated in his appointment as Marshal of France in 1356, a testament to his tactical skill and loyalty, especially following the catastrophic French defeat at the Battle of Poitiers. In this capacity, he played a crucial role in the chevauchées and counter-offensives that characterized the conflict, often employing guerrilla tactics and scorched-earth strategies to disrupt English supply lines and morale.

His contributions were vital in maintaining a degree of French resistance during periods of severe royal weakness and internal strife, particularly under King John II and the regency of the future Charles V. Boucicaut's efforts helped to preserve key French territories and fortresses, laying groundwork for the later reconquests under Charles V. His legacy was further cemented by his son, Jean II Le Meingre, also known as Boucicaut, who inherited his father's martial reputation and achieved even greater renown as a Marshal of France and a crusader."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-22T23:10:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45081590684", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1350"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1350"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Hundred Years' War created a constant demand for skilled military commanders to defend the French kingdom.", "His noble birth into the Le Meingre family provided him with the necessary social standing and resources to pursue a military career.", "The strategic necessity for France to develop effective military leadership after early defeats fostered the rise of figures like Boucicaut."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-22T23:10:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45081590684", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1367-03-23"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "1367-03-23"}], "newValue": ["Jean I Le Meingre's military actions helped to stabilize French defenses during a particularly challenging phase of the Hundred Years' War.", "His distinguished service as Marshal of France solidified the prestige and influence of the